tion  of art design and Technology when the  three of them kind of hit you know like  in a monster or a scare right that's  when video games are at their best  through this horror engineering Striking  Distance hopes to ensure that Callisto  remains unpredictable to every player  resisting the well-worn patterns and  rhythms of survival horror that many of  us are so familiar with in essence Glenn  Schofield hopes that as a die-hard  horror fan he's in the perfect position  to pull the rug out from other horror  veterans when it comes to putting setups  together there are no rules the  setup itself may be that harder  engineering but where it happens and how  it happens is from the gut right you  know I had someone say at EA when I was  making Dead Space they're like hey we're  gonna now that we see how good it is  we're going to give you some extra money  to to make a scare system if they aren't  scared in like 10 minutes and we'll have  something come out and scare them I'm  like that is the exact opposite of what  I want I want to scare you and then you  go  all right and then I give you five  minutes and we scare you again or I'm  gonna scare you and two seconds later  I'm gonna get you again because you'll  never think that we'll do it and then  once you know we're gonna mess with you  now we got you and that's why we do  things like at the beginning of the game  we might scare you out a door or do  something in an elevator right because  then you're always going to be like you  know hyper Vigilant  if you're not a horror fan being  confronted with a game where so much  effort is being put into creating as  scary an experience as possible might  sound like a total nightmare but as  we've covered previously on Mind Games  some studies suggest that experiencing  recreational fear through horror media  has benefits for developing your skills  in emotional regulation if you're like  me and you love horror that theoretical  benefit you get from horror might sound  familiar oh I think it's like a roller  coaster ride I think they uh they like  that adrenaline rush you know that  anticipation and then like uh you know  it's coming but you don't know exactly  when when you're gonna hit that Crest  you don't really go through scares for  10 hours in life right and this is just  a chance to tap into a much different  set of emotions when I'm about to watch  a movie a scary movie there's some days  I'm like no not tonight actually you  know because sometimes horror you got to  get into it right I think that's the way  with uh with the game like that and with  this one we are really trying to immerse  you in it like we spend so much time  energy money on that main character and  the other characters so that the eyes  are right so the teeth are right so they  there's nothing to take you out of the  situation you're in that's why we spent  so much time on the the backgrounds to  try and just make you feel like you're  there if you're a horror veteran the  Callisto protocol may prove to be a  useful experience in that it's a game  aiming to undermine the expectations of  those very players who think they know  exactly what awaits them it's a love  letter to the genre and to those players  who enjoy being scared just make sure  you've got a strong stomach  ah  roller coasters  yeah  um and let me tell you sometimes a jump  scare will get me what I'm not expecting  it  um you know I love it when the camera's  over there  and I'm like okay where are they gonna  show it and then it gets me from  someplace else  um that that kind of scares me

